                "text": "I'm testing which squares get transformed by checking specific row/column patterns across examples, but the results seem inconsistent between example 1 and example 4 \u2014 one favors certain rows becoming 8s while the other favors the opposite. I'm trying to figure out if there's some alternating or positional rule that determines which squares stay as 2s versus which convert to 8s.\n\nThen I sort the squares by their leftmost column position across examples, and something clicks: the colors alternate perfectly in that order \u2014 8, 2, 8, 2, 8, 2, 8. I check this against example 1's squares sorted by column and it looks like the same alternating pattern holds.\n\nBut then testing example 4 breaks it \u2014 sorting by column doesn't reproduce the actual output colors there, so the alternation-by-column theory falls apart and I need another approach.\n\nTrying row-based grouping instead: in example 4, top-row squares end up 2 and lower ones 8, but that's reversed from example 1's pattern, so that doesn't hold either. Maybe it's about which squares touch or overlap something else entirely rather than simple position sorting.\n\nRechecking ex4 more carefully, I list out the starting columns of each square by row and sort them: 1,2,4,7,8,10,11,14 with values alternating 2,8,2,8,2,8,2,8 \u2014 that matches! My earlier read was wrong, and the pattern actually holds based on sorted column position.\n\nTesting ex5 the same way, sorting the leftmost columns gives 0,1,3,5,8,11,12,15 with alternating 2,8,2,8,2,8,2,8, which also matches the expected output. But comparing across examples, the starting value differs \u2014 ex1 and ex3 start with 8, ex2 also starts with 8, while ex5 starts with 2 \u2014 so it's not simply alternating from a fixed start. I wonder if it depends on the parity of the column index itself: in ex1, even columns (0,4) are 8 and odd (1) is 2, which fits a column-parity rule rather than just sequential alternation.\n\n",
